  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Light rain and snow ends through the morning, though \nclouds remain through tonight.\n\nThe last shortwave trough around a departing mid level low is now \ncrossing our region, promoting upslope light rain or drizzle/snow \naround our area. Behind this trough, the lack of lift this afternoon \nwill allow for precipitation to end. With a lingering layer of \nmoisture between 2k and 6k feet, and well below the snow DGZ, we're \nnot expecting much if any additional snow accumulation this morning. \n \nThough precipitation will end across our higher terrain by mid-day, \na layer of stratus will linger right through tonight. This will \nallow for afternoon highs to continue to run below normal, and \ntonight's lows to not drop too much...with the forecast siding above \nmodel guidance tonight for temperatures.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather will return by Monday, with temperatures \ntrending notably warmer by midweek.\n\nAn expansive and strengthening area of surface high will build from \nthe Plains and across the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley today and into \nthe early week. This feature will bring a return to dry conditions, \nwith a few partly to mostly sunny days. Temperatures will remain \ncool ahead of the surface high, but as this surface high slides \neastward, and a stronger southerly flow ensues, afternoon \ntemperatures will rise back into the lower to mid 50s by \nTuesday...and then starting Wednesday a few spots will reach into \nthe 60s, with more and more areas hitting that 60F degree mark by \nthe end of the week.",
